{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1795543753,"nonce":"fkHKJgo6y16Ts74jVSUd4460LMI=","target":"ce2ffa7a0052f271a772b8e5606e1e2b128ddca3f4b46ad92675b21da334a203","message":"`tests/test_fdm.py::test_repeated_march_is_traced_once[None]` failed intermittently on the GPU: two solves of the same transient fdm problem were not bit-identical. Expected: equal results from a cached traced march; only round-off differs.\n\n**Reproduce** Run the test on an RTX 3070 with JAX 0.10.1: it failed 1 of 5 runs alone, and in 2 of 3 larger runs. The trace count was right; the arrays differed at round-off, because GPU reductions run in a different order. On the CPU it passes. The two failing larger runs were on the tree with the `.inside` change (commit b1cddeed in #345); the one passing larger run was on the commit before. The single-test runs (1 of 5 failing alone, 4 of 4 passing after `tests/test_fvm_inside.py`) were on the new tree. The earlier tree was never run 5 times, so the `.inside` change was not shown to matter; the order of reductions is the cause.\n\n**Cause** The test asserted `np.array_equal(first, again)`.\n\n**Fix** Commit c3db3301 in #345 compares to a tolerance: `np.testing.assert_allclose(again, first, rtol=0, atol=1e-14 * np.abs(first).max())`. The bound allows a few ulps; the observed difference was one ulp (1.1e-16 relative) in 4 of 10 repeats. The test still asserts that the second solve adds no trace, which is its purpose.","files":null}]}